    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kampala"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kampala.jpg" alt="Kampala" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Urban_Rising,_KAMPALA,_Uganda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chapelle musa, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buganda began as a small kingdom on the northern and north-western shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>, around the hills where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a> stands today. Its royal traditions, recorded in the late nineteenth century by the chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apolo-kagwa">Apolo Kagwa</a> and others, trace the dynasty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kintu">Kintu</a>, the first man and founding hero, and to Kato Kimera, a prince linked in some versions with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babiito-dynasty">Babito</a> rulers of Bunyoro. Historians treat these accounts as dynastic charters and date the emergence of the kingdom roughly to the fourteenth or fifteenth century, when a group of clans in the core counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/busiro">Busiro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyadondo">Kyadondo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawokota">Mawokota</a> came to accept a common ruler, the <em>kabaka</em>. For several centuries Buganda remained a minor state in the shadow of Bunyoro-Kitara, the dominant power of the region, and its kings fought frequent wars against Bunyoro and its other neighbours. From the seventeenth century, however, the kabakas began to expand, absorbing territories such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butambala">Butambala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gomba">Gomba</a> and Singo. In the early eighteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawanda-of-buganda">Mawanda</a> extended the kingdom eastwards, and in the late eighteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junju-of-buganda">Junju</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semakookiro">Semakookiro</a> conquered the rich province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddu">Buddu</a> from Bunyoro, giving Buganda access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a> and trade of the south-west and making it the leading power on the lake.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke.jpg" alt="John Hanning Speke" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke">John Hanning Speke</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JH_Speke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Southwell Brothers, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buganda’s prosperity rested on the banana plantain, or <em>matooke</em>, grown in permanent gardens that produced food all year round with relatively little labour. Women did most of the cultivation, which freed men for warfare, building, road-making and service to chiefs and the kabaka. This surplus of male labour helped Buganda build roads, palaces and fleets on a scale that impressed nineteenth-century European visitors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hanning-speke">John Hanning Speke</a>, who arrived in 1862. The kingdom was famous for its barkcloth, made by beating the inner bark of fig trees, which served as clothing and a valuable trade good. Society was organised into patrilineal clans, each with a totem and with hereditary heads called <em>bataka</em>, but status depended increasingly on service to the kabaka rather than on birth. Enslaved captives from raids on neighbouring peoples worked in households and gardens. From the 1840s Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> traders from Zanzibar reached the court, exchanging cloth, guns and luxury goods for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and slaves. Traditional religion centred on the <em>lubaale</em> spirits and ancestral shrines, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and, from 1877, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread rapidly among the chiefs and the young pages at court, beginning a religious transformation that made Buganda a heavily Christian society by the early twentieth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard.jpg" alt="Frederick Lugard" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LordLugard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elliott &amp; Fry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buganda’s expansion was achieved by large armies raised by the kabaka through his chiefs, which campaigned almost every year against Bunyoro, Busoga, Ankole and the smaller states and peoples around the kingdom. Warriors fought mainly with spears and shields, and by the mid-nineteenth century the court had acquired growing numbers of firearms from Zanzibari traders, distributed to royal regiments. A distinctive feature was the fleet of large plank-built war canoes on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>, commanded by an admiral called the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabunga">Gabunga</a></em>, which allowed Buganda to raid and dominate the islands and shores of the lake, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sese-islands">Sese Islands</a> and parts of Busoga. Raids brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, slaves and tribute, which the kabaka redistributed to loyal chiefs. In the 1870s and 1880s Buganda faced a revived Bunyoro under Omukama <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabalega">Kabalega</a>, whose armed forces resisted its advance. During the religious wars of 1888–1892, armed Protestant, Catholic and Muslim factions fought for control of the kingdom, and in January 1892 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mengo">Battle of Mengo</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a> of the Imperial British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> Company backed the Protestants with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxim-gun">Maxim gun</a>, decided the issue. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baganda">Ganda</a> forces then fought alongside the British against Bunyoro, and the Ganda general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semei-kakungulu">Semei Kakungulu</a> led the conquest of much of eastern Uganda.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harry-johnston.jpg" alt="Harry Johnston" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Harry Johnston</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Harry_Johnston.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elliott &amp; Fry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buganda was the most centralised of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> kingdoms. The kabaka held supreme authority and was surrounded by elaborate ceremony; because royal princes belonged to their mothers’ clans, any clan could hope to provide a future king, which tied the clans to the monarchy. Over the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries the kabakas reduced the power of the hereditary clan heads by appointing their own chiefs to govern the counties, or <em>saza</em>, and by creating a class of <em>batongole</em> chiefs directly loyal to the throne, who could be promoted or dismissed at will. The <em>katikkiro</em>, or chief minister, headed the administration, and a council of chiefs, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lukiiko">Lukiiko</a></em>, advised the king and heard important cases. Young men served as pages at the royal court, a training ground for future chiefs. Succession was not fixed, and a new kabaka was chosen from among the princes, often after violent rivalry. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buganda-agreement">Buganda Agreement</a> of 1900, negotiated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-johnston">Sir Harry Johnston</a> with the regents of the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daudi-chwa-ii">Daudi Chwa II</a>, led by Katikkiro <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apolo-kagwa">Apolo Kagwa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> recognised the kabaka, the Lukiiko and the chiefs, and distributed large estates of freehold <em>mailo</em> land to the chiefs and the royal family, entrenching a powerful landed Protestant and Catholic elite.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kasubi-tombs"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kasubi-tombs.jpg" alt="Kasubi Tombs" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kasubi-tombs">Kasubi Tombs</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kampala_Kasubi_Tombs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">not not phil from SF, CA, US, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buganda shaped the history of the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> region and of modern Uganda. Its expansion transformed the balance of power around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>, displacing Bunyoro as the dominant kingdom. Its open court made it the gateway through which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> entered the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutesa-i-of-buganda">Mutesa I</a> received Arab traders and, after the journalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> publicised his interest in 1875, Protestant and Catholic missionaries. The execution of young Christian converts on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii">Mwanga II</a> in 1885–1887, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-martyrs">Uganda Martyrs</a>, many of whom died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namugongo">Namugongo</a> in June 1886, gave Uganda some of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>’s best-known saints, canonised by the Catholic Church in 1964. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> extended its rule, it treated Buganda as a partner and used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baganda">Ganda</a> chiefs and agents to administer other regions, so the Uganda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> took its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> form of Buganda, and Ganda institutions, language and personnel spread across the country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luganda">Luganda</a> remains Uganda’s most widely spoken indigenous language. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasubi-tombs">Kasubi Tombs</a>, burial place of four kabakas, were made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2001. Buganda’s demands for autonomy also became one of the central issues of Ugandan politics before and after independence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion.jpg" alt="French Wars of Religion" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_masacre_de_San_Bartolom%C3%A9,_por_Fran%C3%A7ois_Dubois.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">François Dubois 1529-1584, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Buganda lost its independence in stages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">Religious civil war</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii">Mwanga II</a> opened the way to British intervention, and in 1894 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>; Mwanga’s revolt in 1897 failed, and he was deposed and exiled. The 1900 Agreement preserved the monarchy as a privileged province within the Uganda Protectorate, and Buganda prospered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, but real power lay with the colonial government. In 1953 Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-cohen">Andrew Cohen</a> deported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutesa-ii-of-buganda">Mutesa II</a> after he refused to cooperate with British plans for a unitary Uganda; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> provoked a crisis, and the kabaka returned in 1955 as a constitutional monarch. At independence in 1962 Buganda received federal status, and its party, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-yekka">Kabaka Yekka</a>, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-obote">Milton Obote</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uganda-peoples-congress">Uganda People’s Congress</a>; Mutesa II became president of Uganda in 1963. The alliance broke down over the ‘lost counties’ returned to Bunyoro by referendum in 1964 and over Obote’s centralising policies. In May 1966 troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a> stormed the kabaka’s palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mengo">Mengo</a>, and Mutesa II fled to Britain, where he died in 1969. The 1967 constitution abolished all of Uganda’s kingdoms. Under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni">Yoweri Museveni</a> the monarchy was restored as a cultural institution, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-muwenda-mutebi-ii">Ronald Muwenda Mutebi II</a> was crowned in 1993.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Buganda grew from a small lakeside kingdom into the most centralised state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> region, became the gateway for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, gave its name and many institutions to Uganda, and remains a powerful symbol of identity as a restored cultural monarchy.</em></p>

